  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 126 PM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery shows two weak upper level troughs \nto the southwest. The first trough is just south of the Four \nCorners and the second is near Yuma, Arizona. The first trough \npasses to our south tonight bringing high clouds. A few weak \nshowers will\u00a0be possible\u00a0across southwest Colorado, but nothing is\nexpected in our forecast area. Temperatures will be slightly \ncooler Monday due to increased cloud cover and slight cooling \nbehind a very weak cold front that backed south across the area\ntoday. The central mountains could see isolated rain and snow \nshowers Monday afternoon and evening, otherwise dry conditions \nwill continue. Tuesday will warm a little, and we'll see a \ncontinued chance for isolated showers over the central mountains \nduring the afternoon and evening. An upper level ridge forms over \nthe region Tuesday night and Wednesday ahead of an upper level low\nalong the west coast. We'll see mild temperatures with highs in \nthe 70s over northeast Colorado and dry conditions across the \narea.\n\nThe upper level ridge shifts east of Colorado Wednesday night as \nthe upper level low along the west coast moves inland over \nCalifornia. This will bring a southwest flow aloft to Colorado. \nThe flow extends far enough south to pick up subtropical \nmoisture.\u00a0On Thursday, the upper level ridge will remain over the\nmiddle part of the country. This\u00a0will cause the upper level low \nto track northeast across the intermountain west Thursday \nand\u00a0Friday. By late Friday the upper level low (in a much weaker \nstate) is expected to be north to northwest of Colorado, somewhere\nover Wyoming and/or Idaho. Southwest flow aloft and moisture will\nincrease over western Colorado under this pattern on Thursday. \nWidespread rain and mountain snow are expected over the southwest \nColorado mountains. The moisture embedded in the southwest\u00a0flow \naloft is wrung out over the mountains with only scattered showers \nexpected for the north central mountains and dry conditions east \nof the mountains through Friday.\n\nFor next weekend, models still vary on solutions, but agree there\nwon't be much left of the upper level low. There is loose \nagreement that unorganized troughiness passes eastward across the \nRockies, but some models even wash this troughiness out, leaving \nonly westerly flow aloft. Temperatures likely cool closer to \nnormal. Regarding precipitation chances, model spread ranges from \ndry conditions to a round of light rain and mountain snow \nshowers.",
